    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d408">

                <pb facs="0480" n="474" xml:id="pg474"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.458/22a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Roosevelt to
                        the President of the Presidium of the Supreme Council of the Soviet
                        Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kalinin</hi>)<anchor n="55" xml:id="d408-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-11-06">November 6,
                        1942</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>On the occasion of this twenty-fifth anniversary of the establishment of the
                    Soviet State, I convey to Your Excellency the congratulations of the Government
                    and people of the United States.</p>
                <p>For the second time in a generation, our two countries are in the forefront of a
                    gathering of nations aligned against a common enemy. Collaboration in the mighty
                    military task before us must be the prelude to collaboration in the mightier
                    task of creating a world at peace.</p>
                <p>The resistance of free peoples has made possible the mounting power of the United
                    Nations. The Russian Army and the Russian people in their continuing struggle
                    against Nazi conquest today bear the brunt of the massed weight of the Nazi
                    might and their incomparable heroism stands as a symbol of determination and
                    unrelenting effort.</p>
                <p>Let Your Excellency rest assured that the steadily growing power of the United
                    States has been, and will continue to be, dedicated to complete victory.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Franklin D. Roosevelt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d408-f1" n="55" type="source" xml:id="d408-f1-content">
                <p>This telegram was sent directly to Moscow, and a copy was mailed to Kuibyshev on
                    November 20, 1942. For the resumption of the practice of sending a message of
                    felicitation on the occasion of the anniversary of the October Revolution, see
                        <ref target="frus1941v01#pg_654">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1941, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 654</ref>, footnote 26.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
